'''''Griselinia lucida''''', commonly known as '''puka''', '''akapuka''', and '''shining broadleaf''', is a species of shrub in the family [[Griseliniaceae]]. It is [[endemic]] to New Zealand. Its [[Species distribution|range]] mainly covers the [[North Island|North]] and [[South Island]]s. It typically grows [[epiphytic]]ally: the term 'epiphytically', means it grows on other plants, although it can also be found in coastal and rocky environments. It reaches {{convert|10|m|abbr=off|sigfig=1}} in maximum height. The leaves are large, thick, and glossy, and the [[root]]s are grooved and often grow downward until they reach the forest floor.

''Griselinia lucida'' was first [[Species description|described]] by the German botanist [[Georg Forster]] in 1786. It is only one of two ''[[Griselinia]]'' species found in New Zealand. Pollination is likely achieved by insects or the wind. The fruits are adapted to being [[Biological dispersal|dispersed]] by [[Frugivore|fruit-eating animals]] (frugivores), such as birds. Germination requires light and moist conditions. Its 2023 [[conservation status]] in the [[New Zealand Threat Classification System]] is "Not Threatened".

==Description==
{{Stack|float=right|
[[File:Griselinia lucida 13738540.jpg|thumb|right|Developing fruits of ''G. lucida''|alt=Refer to caption]]
}}
''Griselinia lucida'' is a species of [[evergreen]] shrub or small tree in the family [[Griseliniaceae]]. It reaches {{convert|8|m|abbr=off}} in height, and it commonly occurs as an [[epiphyte]] (growing on another plant).{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}}{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=1}} The [[Crown (botany)|crown]] can reach a width of {{convert|8|m|abbr=on}} or more in a [[Canopy (biology)|canopy]]. ''G. lucida'' can be classified as a [[hemiepiphyte]] because of its large, brown to grey-coloured grooved [[root]]s, which often grow downward to reach the forest floor.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=1}}{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|p=265}}

Leaves are thick, glabrous, glossy, and leathery in texture. They are found in an alternating pattern and are broadly ovate to oblong in character, with a rounded tip. Leaves are {{convert|7–18|cm|abbr=on}} long, {{convert|5–9|cm|abbr=on}} wide, and 0.3–0.7&nbsp;mm thick. Very young leaves can have a reddish-purple coloured [[Leaf margin|margin]]. The undersides of the leaves can be whitish in colour. The [[Petiole (botany)|petioles]] are {{convert|2.5–5|cm|abbr=on}} long.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=2}}{{Sfn|Dillon|Munoz-Schick|1993|p=9}}

The [[inflorescence]]s (flower clusters) are found on [[Peduncle (botany)|peduncle]]s, which are {{convert|10-15 |cm|abbr=on}} long. The peduncle can have [[trichome]]s at the top. The inflorescences are found at the ends of branchlets. Flowers are small, only about 1–3&nbsp;mm long, and they are a cream or yellowish colour. Fruiting occurs in [[infructescence]]s (fruit clusters) with small, oval, berry-like, fruits that are 4–10&nbsp;mm long and 5&nbsp;mm in diameter. They are dark purple when they are [[Ripening|ripened]].{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}}{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=2}} Each fruit contains a single seed, which is about 4&nbsp;mm long and 3&nbsp;mm wide.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=2}} ''G. lucida'' produces [[glucoside]]s which may help attract insects.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=13}} ''G. lucida'' has a [[diploid]] chromosome count of 36.{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}}

==Taxonomy==
''Griselinia lucida'' was first [[Species description|described]] by the German botanist [[Georg Forster]] in 1786.{{Sfn|IPNI|2026}}{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=6}} There are only two New Zealand members of the [[genus]], ''G. lucida'' and ''[[Griselinia littoralis|G. littoralis]]''. The taxonomic placement of this genus was previously uncertain, and has been formerly placed in the families [[Araliaceae]], [[Cornaceae]], and [[Juglandaceae]]. A 1980 study placed it in [[Griseliniaceae]]. ''Griselinia'' is suggested to have emerged in the [[Miocene]].{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=5}}

===Etymology===
The [[etymology]] (word origin) of ''G. lucida''{{'}}s genus name, ''Griselinia'', is named is honour of the Italian botanist {{Interlanguage link|Francesco Griselini|it}}.{{Sfn|Gledhill|2002|p=151}} The [[specific epithet]] (second part of the scientific name), ''lucida'', means 'shining'.{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}} The species is [[Common name|commonly known]] as puka, akapuka and shining broadleaf. Other [[Māori language|Māori]] names for the plant include {{lang|mi|akakōpuka}} and {{lang|mi|pukatea}}.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=6}}{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}} The name 'puka' can also be applied to the unrelated ''[[Meryta sinclairii]]''.{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=58}}

==Ecology==
[[File:Silvereye Jan 2010.jpg|thumb|right|alt=A silvereye bird perched on a branch|The native silvereye bird consumes the fruits of ''G. lucida''.]]
''Griselinia lucida''{{'}}s seeds are [[Biological dispersal|dispersed]] by [[Frugivore|fruit-eating animals]] (frugivores), such as birds.{{Sfn|Burrows|1999|p=2}}{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=6}} ''G. lucida''{{'}}s fruits are well-adapted to be dispersed by bird species, which includes, but is not limited to: [[kererū]] (''Hemiphaga novaeseelandiae''), [[New Zealand bellbird]]s (''Anthornis melanura''), [[silvereyes]] (''Zosterops lateralis''), and [[tūī]] (''Prosthemadera novaeseelandiae'').{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=6}} The [[North Island kōkako]] (''Callaeas wilsoni'') is known to instead browse the leaves and [[bud]]s of the tree, and may also consume the fruits.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=13}}{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=58}} Pollination is likely achieved by insects or the wind.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=6}} Bees occasionally visit the flowers to collect [[nectar]].{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=58}}{{Sfn|Butz Huryn|1995|pp=6, 10}} Germination of the seed requires light and moist conditions.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=6}}

''Griselinia lucida'' plays [[Host (biology)|host]] to numerous insects, including: ''[[Aenetus virescens]]'', ''[[Glaucias amyoti]]'', ''[[Heterocrossa gonosemana]]'', and ''[[Phyllonorycter messaniella]]''. On [[Rangitoto Island]], the introduced [[brush-tailed rock-wallaby]] (''Petrogale penicillata'') fed on ''G. lucida'' up until their eradication in the late 1990s. ''G. lucida'' is not a preferred food source for introduced [[Common brushtail possum in New Zealand|common brushtail possum]] (''Trichosurus vulpecula'').{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=14}} Deer and goats possibly eat parts of the tree, but this has not been reported in published studies.{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=58}}{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=14}}

==Distribution==
[[File:Griselinia lucida growing on scoria on Rangitoto Island.jpg|thumb|right|alt=Griselinia lucida growing in a rocky environment|''Griselinia lucida'' growing in a rocky environment on [[Rangitoto Island]]]]
''Griselinia lucida'' is [[endemic]] to New Zealand. Its [[Species distribution|
range]] mainly covers the [[North Island|North]], and [[South Island]]s, but the species is most common in the North Island.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=7}} ''G. lucida'' is primarily found on the west coast of New Zealand, with a relatively small presence on the east coasts of both islands. In the North Island, ''G. lucida'' is able to extend further inland than the South Island due to there being less mountainous terrain and warmer climates.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|pp=6–7}} The species' southern limit is confirmed to be at least [[Milford Sound]] in [[Fiordland]]. Records further south of Milford Sound are uncertain, as they are not backed by [[herbarium]] specimens.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|pp=7, 13}} ''G. lucida''{{'}}s 2023 [[conservation status]] in the [[New Zealand Threat Classification System]] is "Not Threatened".{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}}

===Habitat===
''Griselinia lucida'' can establish itself in different environments. ''G. lucida'' primarily grows as a light-demanding epiphyte in the canopy of [[old-growth forest]]s, but it also occurs on coastal cliffs and rocky sites.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=8}}{{Sfn|Moore|1978|p=102}} In the North Island's [[Taranaki]] and [[Waikato]] regions, ''G. lucida'' is most common in epiphytic relationships with  [[Laurelia novae-zelandiae|pukatea]] (''Laurelia novae-zelandiae'') and [[Beilschmiedia tawa|tawa]] (''Beilschmiedia tawa'').{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=11}} Near the South Island coastal town of [[Kaikōura]], ''G. lucida'' is commonly associated with [[Freycinetia banksii|kiekie]] (''Freycinetia banksii''),  [[Rhopalostylis sapida|nīkau]] (''Rhopalostylis sapida''), among other plants.{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=13}}

==Uses==
The indigenous [[Māori people]] had limited uses of ''G. lucida'', but they did use the wood in the making of cartridge holders.{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|p=265}} In [[Rongoā|traditional medicinal practices]], the inner bark is said to have soothing characteristics for skin conditions. The inner bark is similar to that found on ''G. littoralis''.{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|p=265}}{{Sfn|Bryan|Clarkson|Clearwater|2011|p=14}} [[European settlers in New Zealand|European settlers]] used the wood in constructing fence-posts and in work by [[millwright]]s.{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=58}}{{Sfn|Kirk|1889|p=188}}

